body:
Digging into it a bit more, I'm having second thoughts on the correct way of proceeding here. The thing is that it breaks due to the optimisation we've chosen to do, that is, the generated code seems to be different for the 2 following cases:
* ` @for(item of items; track trackFn($index, item))` will generate `repeaterCreate(1, WithALoop_For_2_Template, 2, 1, "span", null, ctx.trackFn)` - notice that here we pass a reference to a function assuming that it is defined on the context;
` @for(item of items; track trackFn($index, item) + 5)` (notice the expression in the track by) will generate `repeaterCreate(1, WithALoop_For_2_Template, 2, 1, "span", null, _forTrack0, true)` where `_forTrack0` is defined as:

```javascript
function _forTrack0($index, $item) {
  return this.trackFn($index, $item) + 5;
}
```
(see https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-xgaggq)

So the optimisation we've got is really good for common cases but I can see how it could get surprising. Essentially we are trading the "correctness" for perf here. I still do think that this is the right tradeoff but leaning towards keeping this issue open and see how many people bump into it.

@SuNNjek what I was trying to explain is that with the current implementation a reference to the `trackBy` function must be defined when the component is _created_ - which is much earlier, before `@Input`s are set. 

In your particular case your code is correct but you are bumping into a slightly separate issue - the `this` context is not present when the `trackItem` is invoked. 

The combination of the two mentioned issues makes your code to fail in the end but you can work-around it by defining the `trackItem` function as an arrow function: 

```typescript
protected trackItem = <T>(index: number, item: T) => {
    return this.trackBy?.(index, item) ?? index;
  };
```

(live code at: https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-ijejgx?file=src%2Fmain.ts)

Having said this I do recognise that this is very non-obvious behaviour and we will look into improving support for this scenario. For now the presented work-around should do the trick.
